# Starter Questions & Answers, J–Z.
FAQ = [
('Jesus', 'Is Jesus God or man?',
 'Both. Jesus Christ is true God and true man, one divine Person with a divine nature and a human nature. He did not stop being God when he became man, and he truly lived, suffered, died and rose as one of us.',
 'CCC 464–469; John 1:1, 14; Colossians 2:9'),
('Jesus', 'Why did Jesus have to die?',
 'Out of love, the Father sent his Son to reconcile us to himself. Jesus freely offered his life on the cross as a perfect sacrifice for the sins of the whole world, conquering sin and death and opening the way to eternal life.',
 'CCC 599–618; John 3:16; Romans 5:8; 1 Peter 2:24'),
('Joseph', 'Why is Saint Joseph so important?',
 'Joseph, the husband of Mary, was chosen by God to be the foster father of Jesus and guardian of the Holy Family. A just and faithful man, he obeyed God promptly. He is patron of the universal Church, of fathers, workers and a happy death. His feasts are 19 March and 1 May.',
 'CCC 437, 532; Matthew 1:18–25; 2:13–15'),
('Judgement', 'What happens when we die?',
 'At death the soul is separated from the body, and each person receives an immediate particular judgement: entrance into heaven, through purification (purgatory) if needed, or eternal separation from God (hell). At the end of time, at the Last Judgement, the dead will rise and Christ will come in glory.',
 'CCC 1021–1022, 1038–1041; Hebrews 9:27'),
('Kingdom of God', 'What is the Kingdom of God?',
 'The Kingdom of God is God’s reign of love, justice and peace, which Jesus announced and made present by his words, miracles and above all his Death and Resurrection. The Church is its seed and beginning on earth; it will come in fullness at the end of time.',
 'CCC 541–556, 2816–2821; Mark 1:15'),
('Kneeling', 'Why do Catholics kneel in church?',
 'Kneeling is a sign of humility, adoration and penitence before God. Catholics kneel during the Eucharistic Prayer, when the bread and wine become the Body and Blood of Christ, and often after Holy Communion.',
 'Psalm 95:6; Philippians 2:10'),
('Lent', 'What is Lent?',
 'Lent is the forty days of preparation for Easter, from Ash Wednesday to the Mass of the Lord’s Supper on Holy Thursday. It recalls Jesus’ forty days in the desert. Through prayer, fasting and almsgiving we turn back to God and prepare to renew our baptismal promises at Easter.',
 'CCC 540, 1095, 1438; Matthew 4:1–2; 6:1–18'),
('Limbo', 'Does the Church teach Limbo?',
 'Limbo was a theological opinion, never a defined doctrine. For children who die without Baptism, the Church entrusts them to the mercy of God, who desires all to be saved, and we may hope there is a way of salvation for them.',
 'CCC 1261; 1 Timothy 2:4; International Theological Commission (2007)'),
('Liturgical Colours', 'What do the colours of the priest’s vestments mean?',
 'Green is used in Ordinary Time (hope and growth); violet in Advent and Lent (preparation and penance); white or gold at Christmas, Easter and feasts of the Lord, Mary and non-martyr saints (joy and purity); red on Palm Sunday, Good Friday, Pentecost and feasts of martyrs and apostles (the Spirit and blood); rose on Gaudete and Laetare Sundays.',
 'General Instruction of the Roman Missal 346'),
('Liturgy', 'What is the liturgy?',
 'The liturgy is the official public worship of the Church, above all the Mass and the sacraments, and also the Liturgy of the Hours. In it Christ continues the work of our redemption, and we join him in praising the Father.',
 'CCC 1066–1075; Sacrosanctum Concilium 7'),
('Lord’s Prayer', 'Why is the Our Father so important?',
 'It is the prayer Jesus himself taught his disciples. Tertullian called it “the summary of the whole Gospel.” It has seven petitions: three for God’s glory and four for our needs. It is prayed at every Mass.',
 'CCC 2759–2865; Matthew 6:9–13; Luke 11:2–4'),
('Marriage', 'Why is marriage a sacrament?',
 'Christ raised marriage between a baptised man and woman to the dignity of a sacrament. Their covenant of lifelong, faithful and fruitful love becomes a sign of Christ’s love for his Church, and they receive grace to love each other and raise their children in faith.',
 'CCC 1601–1666; Ephesians 5:25–32; Matthew 19:4–6'),
('Mary', 'Why do Catholics honour Mary?',
 'Mary is the Mother of Jesus, who is God, so she is the Mother of God. She is the first and most perfect disciple, full of grace. Catholics honour her as Scripture foretold: “all generations will call me blessed.” We do not worship her; we love her and ask her prayers, because she always leads us to her Son.',
 'CCC 963–975, 495; Luke 1:42–48; John 2:5'),
('Mary', 'Why is Mary called the Mother of God?',
 'Because her Son, Jesus, is truly God. The Council of Ephesus in 431 confirmed this title (Theotokos, “God-bearer”). Elizabeth greeted her as “the mother of my Lord.” The title protects the truth that Jesus is one Person, God and man.',
 'CCC 495, 509; Luke 1:43'),
('Mary', 'Did Mary have other children?',
 'The Church teaches that Mary remained a virgin all her life. The “brothers” of Jesus mentioned in the Gospels are close relatives; in Hebrew and Aramaic the same word was used for cousins and kin. James and Joses, called his brothers, were sons of another Mary.',
 'CCC 499–501; Matthew 13:55; 27:56'),
('Mass', 'What happens at Mass?',
 'The Mass makes present the one sacrifice of Christ on the cross and the meal of the Last Supper. It has two main parts: the Liturgy of the Word, where God speaks to us in Scripture, and the Liturgy of the Eucharist, where bread and wine become the Body and Blood of Christ and we receive him in Communion.',
 'CCC 1345–1355, 1365–1367; Luke 22:19'),
('Mass', 'Why must Catholics go to Mass on Sunday?',
 'Sunday is the day of the Resurrection, the Lord’s Day. The third Commandment tells us to keep it holy, and from the beginning Christians gathered on the first day of the week for the Eucharist. Deliberately missing Sunday Mass without a serious reason, such as illness, is a grave sin.',
 'CCC 2174–2183; Acts 20:7; Hebrews 10:25'),
('Miracles', 'Do miracles still happen?',
 'Yes. God can and does act beyond the ordinary course of nature, often in answer to prayer. The Church investigates claimed miracles very carefully, with doctors and scientists, for example before a saint is beatified or canonised.',
 'CCC 547–550; Mark 16:17–18'),
('Mortal Sin', 'What is a mortal sin?',
 'A mortal sin destroys charity in the heart. Three conditions must be met together: the matter is grave, the person knows it is seriously wrong, and the person freely chooses it. It must be confessed in the sacrament of Reconciliation.',
 'CCC 1855–1861; 1 John 5:16–17'),
('Mortal Sin', 'What is the difference between mortal and venial sin?',
 'Venial sin is a less serious sin, or a serious matter without full knowledge or full consent. It weakens charity but does not break our friendship with God. Mortal sin breaks it. Venial sins are forgiven in many ways, including the Eucharist, but confessing them is strongly recommended.',
 'CCC 1862–1864'),
('Natural Family Planning', 'What does the Church teach about family planning?',
 'Married couples may space births for serious reasons by using the natural rhythms of the woman’s fertility, which respects the meaning of married love. The Church teaches that contraception, which separates love from openness to life, is morally wrong.',
 'CCC 2366–2372; Humanae Vitae 10–16'),
('Nicene Creed', 'What is the Nicene Creed?',
 'It is the profession of faith from the Councils of Nicaea (325) and Constantinople (381), prayed at Sunday Mass. It states clearly that Jesus is “God from God, Light from Light, true God from true God, consubstantial with the Father,” and that the Holy Spirit is “the Lord, the giver of life.”',
 'CCC 195–196'),
('Novena', 'What is a novena?',
 'A novena is nine days of prayer for a special intention or in preparation for a feast. It comes from the nine days the apostles prayed with Mary between the Ascension and Pentecost.',
 'Acts 1:14'),
('Original Sin', 'What is original sin?',
 'Original sin is the state of deprivation of original holiness and justice that all humanity inherits from the sin of our first parents. It is not a personal fault, but it leaves human nature wounded and inclined to sin. Baptism takes it away and gives us God’s life.',
 'CCC 396–409; Genesis 3; Romans 5:12–19'),
('Ordination', 'Why can only men be ordained priests?',
 'Jesus chose only men as his twelve apostles, and the apostles did the same when choosing their successors. The Church considers herself bound by this choice of the Lord. This is not about worth: men and women are equal in dignity, and women have always had indispensable roles in the Church.',
 'CCC 1577; Ordinatio Sacerdotalis (1994)'),
('Pentecost', 'What happened at Pentecost?',
 'Fifty days after Easter, the Holy Spirit came down on the apostles and Mary in the form of tongues of fire. The apostles began to proclaim Jesus boldly, and about three thousand people were baptised. Pentecost is often called the birthday of the Church.',
 'CCC 731–732, 1287; Acts 2:1–41'),
('Pope', 'Why do Catholics have a Pope?',
 'Jesus made Peter the rock of his Church and gave him the keys of the kingdom. The Pope, as Bishop of Rome, is Peter’s successor and the visible source and foundation of unity for the bishops and the whole Church.',
 'CCC 880–882; Matthew 16:18–19; John 21:15–17'),
('Prayer', 'How should I pray?',
 'Prayer is raising our mind and heart to God. Speak to him simply and honestly, listen in silence, read the Scriptures, and use the Church’s prayers. Prayer includes adoration, thanksgiving, sorrow for sin and asking for our needs and others’. Set a regular time each day.',
 'CCC 2558–2565, 2626–2643, 2697–2719; Matthew 6:6'),
('Priests', 'What is the role of a priest?',
 'A priest receives the sacrament of Holy Orders to serve God’s people as a co-worker of the bishop. He celebrates Mass, forgives sins in Confession, anoints the sick, preaches the Word and cares for his people as a shepherd, acting in the person of Christ.',
 'CCC 1562–1568; Hebrews 5:1'),
('Purgatory', 'What is purgatory?',
 'Purgatory is a final purification for those who die in God’s grace and friendship but are still imperfectly purified. They are assured of eternal salvation, but need purification to enter the joy of heaven. We help them by prayer, especially the Mass.',
 'CCC 1030–1032; 2 Maccabees 12:46; 1 Corinthians 3:15'),
('Purgatory', 'Why do Catholics pray for the dead?',
 'Because love does not end at death, and those being purified can be helped by our prayers, almsgiving and above all the Mass. Scripture praises Judas Maccabeus for offering a sacrifice for the dead. November is especially dedicated to praying for the faithful departed.',
 'CCC 958, 1032; 2 Maccabees 12:43–46'),
('Queen of Heaven', 'Why is Mary called Queen of Heaven?',
 'Because she is the Mother of Christ the King and was taken up body and soul into heaven. In the kingdoms of Israel the mother of the king was queen. Her queenship is one of service and intercession, sharing in her Son’s reign. The feast of the Queenship of Mary is 22 August.',
 'CCC 966; 1 Kings 2:19; Revelation 12:1'),
('Real Presence', 'Why do Catholics adore the Eucharist outside Mass?',
 'Because Jesus remains truly present in the consecrated Host, reserved in the tabernacle. Eucharistic adoration is a way to spend time with him, thank him and pray. Jesus asked his friends, “Could you not watch with me one hour?”',
 'CCC 1378–1381; Matthew 26:40'),
('Relics', 'What are relics?',
 'Relics are the bodies or parts of the bodies of saints, or objects they used. Catholics honour (never worship) relics because the saints’ bodies were temples of the Holy Spirit and will rise in glory. Scripture tells of healings through Elisha’s bones and cloths touched by Saint Paul.',
 'CCC 1674; 2 Kings 13:21; Acts 19:11–12'),
('Resurrection', 'Will our bodies rise again?',
 'Yes. We believe in the resurrection of the body. At the end of time, God will raise our bodies and reunite them with our souls, as Christ rose from the dead. The bodies of the just will be glorified.',
 'CCC 988–1019; 1 Corinthians 15:42–44; John 5:28–29'),
('Rosary', 'What is the Rosary?',
 'The Rosary is a prayer in which we meditate on the life of Jesus with Mary, praying the Our Father, ten Hail Marys and the Glory Be for each mystery. There are four sets of mysteries: Joyful, Sorrowful, Glorious and Luminous. Popes have called it a summary of the whole Gospel.',
 'CCC 971, 2678, 2708; Rosarium Virginis Mariae'),
('Rosary', 'Which Rosary mysteries are prayed on each day?',
 'Joyful mysteries on Monday and Saturday; Sorrowful on Tuesday and Friday; Glorious on Wednesday and Sunday; Luminous on Thursday.',
 'Rosarium Virginis Mariae 38'),
('Sacramentals', 'What are sacramentals?',
 'Sacramentals are sacred signs instituted by the Church, such as blessings, holy water, rosaries, medals, scapulars and blessed candles. They do not give grace as the sacraments do, but they prepare us to receive grace and help us sanctify everyday life.',
 'CCC 1667–1679'),
('Sacraments', 'What are the seven sacraments?',
 'Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist (sacraments of initiation); Penance and the Anointing of the Sick (sacraments of healing); Holy Orders and Matrimony (sacraments at the service of communion). They are effective signs of grace, instituted by Christ, by which divine life is given to us.',
 'CCC 1113–1134, 1210'),
('Saints', 'Why do Catholics pray to saints?',
 'We do not pray to saints in place of God. We ask them to pray for us, as we ask friends on earth to pray for us. The saints are alive in Christ and close to God, and “the prayer of a righteous man has great power.”',
 'CCC 956, 2683; James 5:16; Revelation 5:8'),
('Saints', 'How does someone become a saint?',
 'After a person’s death, the Church may study their life. If they lived heroic virtue, they are declared Venerable. With a verified miracle through their intercession they are beatified (Blessed); with a second miracle, canonised (Saint). Martyrs can be beatified without a miracle. The Church does not make saints; it recognises them.',
 'CCC 828'),
('Scapular', 'What is the brown scapular?',
 'The brown scapular is a sacramental of the Carmelite family: two small pieces of cloth worn over the shoulders, a sign of consecration to Mary and of trust in her protection. Tradition links it with Saint Simon Stock (1251). It is not a charm; it is a commitment to live as a Christian.',
 'CCC 1667–1673'),
('Scripture and Tradition', 'Why do Catholics rely on Tradition, not only the Bible?',
 'Before the New Testament was written, the apostles handed on the faith by preaching. The Word of God comes to us through Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition, both from the same divine source. Saint Paul told Christians to hold fast to traditions taught “by word of mouth or by letter.”',
 'CCC 80–83; 2 Thessalonians 2:15; John 21:25'),
('Second Coming', 'Do Catholics believe Jesus will come again?',
 'Yes. Christ will come again in glory to judge the living and the dead, and his Kingdom will have no end. No one knows the day or the hour, so we live ready, watching and praying.',
 'CCC 668–682; Matthew 24:36–44; Acts 1:11'),
('Sin', 'What is sin?',
 'Sin is an offence against God, a failure in genuine love for God and neighbour caused by a perverse attachment to certain goods. It wounds human nature and solidarity. God’s mercy is greater than any sin for those who repent.',
 'CCC 1846–1876; Psalm 51; Luke 15'),
('Sunday', 'Why do Christians worship on Sunday, not Saturday?',
 'Jesus rose from the dead on the first day of the week, Sunday. The first Christians gathered to break bread on that day, the “Lord’s Day,” which fulfils the meaning of the Jewish Sabbath.',
 'CCC 2174–2176; Acts 20:7; Revelation 1:10'),
('Theological Virtues', 'What are faith, hope and charity?',
 'They are the three theological virtues, given by God and directed to God. Faith believes God and what he has revealed; hope desires heaven and trusts in God’s help; charity loves God above all things and our neighbour as ourselves. “The greatest of these is love.”',
 'CCC 1812–1829; 1 Corinthians 13:13'),
('Tithing', 'Must Catholics give money to the Church?',
 'Yes. One of the precepts of the Church is to help provide for the needs of the Church according to one’s ability. There is no fixed percentage for all; giving generously of our time, talents and money is a sign of gratitude to God.',
 'CCC 2043; 2 Corinthians 9:7'),
('Transubstantiation', 'What does transubstantiation mean?',
 'It is the word the Church uses for the change that takes place at the consecration of the Mass: the whole substance of the bread becomes the Body of Christ, and the whole substance of the wine becomes his Blood, while their appearances remain.',
 'CCC 1376; Council of Trent'),
('Trinity', 'What is the Holy Trinity?',
 'The Trinity is the central mystery of Christian faith: one God in three divine Persons, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. They are not three gods but one, each fully God, distinct in their relations. We know this only because God revealed it.',
 'CCC 232–267; Matthew 28:19; 2 Corinthians 13:14'),
('Unity', 'Why is the Church called One, Holy, Catholic and Apostolic?',
 'These are the four marks of the Church. She is one, united in faith, sacraments and leadership; holy, because Christ makes her holy; catholic, meaning universal, sent to all peoples; and apostolic, built on the apostles and their successors.',
 'CCC 811–870'),
('Venial Sin', 'Do I need to confess venial sins?',
 'It is not strictly necessary, but the Church strongly recommends it. Regular confession of everyday faults helps us form our conscience, fight bad tendencies, let ourselves be healed by Christ and grow in the life of the Spirit.',
 'CCC 1458'),
('Virtues', 'What are the cardinal virtues?',
 'Prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance. They are called “cardinal” (from the Latin for hinge) because all other moral virtues hinge on them. They help us live good lives with ease and joy.',
 'CCC 1805–1809; Wisdom 8:7'),
('Vocation', 'What is a vocation?',
 'A vocation is God’s call. Every Christian is called to holiness. Within this, God calls each person to a particular way of life: marriage, the priesthood, religious life or consecrated single life. We discover it through prayer, the sacraments and good guidance.',
 'CCC 1533–1535, 2013; Lumen Gentium 40'),
('Works of Mercy', 'What are the works of mercy?',
 'The corporal works: feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, clothe the naked, shelter the homeless, visit the sick, visit the imprisoned and bury the dead. The spiritual works: counsel the doubtful, instruct the ignorant, admonish sinners, comfort the afflicted, forgive offences, bear wrongs patiently and pray for the living and the dead.',
 'CCC 2447; Matthew 25:31–46'),
('Worship', 'What is the difference between worship and honour?',
 'Worship (adoration, in Latin latria) is given to God alone. The honour given to the saints is called veneration (dulia), and the special honour given to Mary is called hyperdulia. Honouring God’s friends glorifies God, who made them holy.',
 'CCC 2096–2097, 971'),
('Xmas', 'Is it wrong to write “Xmas”?',
 'Not in itself. X is the Greek letter chi, the first letter of Christos, Christ, and Christians have used it as a symbol of Christ for centuries, as in the Chi-Rho (XP). Still, writing “Christmas” in full keeps Christ clearly at the centre.',
 ''),
('Yahweh', 'Why don’t Catholics say “Yahweh” in the liturgy?',
 'Out of reverence. Following ancient Jewish practice, the Church does not pronounce the sacred four-letter name of God (YHWH) in the liturgy, but says “the Lord” instead. In 2008 the Holy See asked that this be observed in prayers and songs at Mass.',
 'Congregation for Divine Worship, letter of 29 June 2008; CCC 209'),
('Zeal', 'What does it mean to have zeal for God?',
 'Zeal is burning love for God and his glory that moves us to serve him and bring others to him. Jesus showed it when he cleansed the Temple: “Zeal for your house will consume me.” Christian zeal is always joined to charity and humility.',
 'John 2:17; Psalm 69:9; Romans 12:11'),
]
